{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/758543-guidebook-to-stygia-chapter-two-entertainment-district","id":758543,"title":"Guidebook to Stygia, Chapter Two: Entertainment District","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-11-06T06:40:58-08:00","id":758543,"title":"Guidebook to Stygia, Chapter Two: Entertainment District","content":"&quot;Crikey! Look at the size of that one! Got way too many teeth in the wrong places, but if you know just the right spot to pet it'll calm right down&quot; the Wraith of Steve Irwin<br /> <br /> &quot;Yes, yes, dance dance, die, die! Ah, I've never understood why the others of my station cannot enjoy a gladiator match as much as I do. Its all the thrill of the clash of blades and thunk of crossbow bolts with none of the messy bits. Oh sure, occasionally one of the fighters goes into a Harrowing after a fight, but that's rare...Relatively speaking.&quot; - The Smiling Lord during a battle in the Arena Mortus<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The Entertainment District<br /> <br /> Wraiths are, at heart, passionate. You have passions that tie you to the Shadowlands, and if you didn't, well you wouldn't be here reading this book. Few are more passionate then artists, and artists have an unfortunate tendency to die young and before their time. Is it any wonder, then, that so many Artists find their way to the docks of Stygia and then into the city proper? Of course, its not JUST artists here, any institution with an entertainment value can be found within the rough area of the Entertainment District, Zoos, brothels, bookshops, gladiator arenas...If you need some time to soothe your jangled nerves and find peace among the dead (Or perhaps to slake some bloodlust), you can find it in the Entertainment District.<br /> <br /> Authors Note: The Stygian Obulus.<br /> <br /> Forged from a single soul, the Obulus is the main currency within Stygia, though micro-economies based on the Barter System are not terribly uncommon, they tend to be shortlived. At any rate, those of you who are new among the Dead are likely used to Dollars, Pounds, Pesos, and other paper-and-coin monies. A single Obulus is worth something in the area of ten American dollars in raw buying power, though this is a general abstraction at best. It is perfectly legal to partition an Obulus to a maximum of ten pieces, each worth around an American Dollar, and through carefully kept trade secrets of the Artificers, any fragment of an Obulus can be merged with any other fragment to reconstitute the whole, creating different 'denominations' of coinage. So a half Obulus is worth around five american dollars, and a quarter around two dollars and fifty cents.<br /> <br /> Its worth noting that the main condition inflation and deflation is determined by is availability of souls. During wars, genocides, and Maelstroms in Stygian Territories, the value of an Obulus goes down as the amount of souls converted into them increases. The reverse is true during times of general peace in the Skinlands. If you have relic money brought over from the Skinlands at the time of your death, the Artificers will usually trade it in for slightly less then its worth in Obols, as the relic money can be reworked into more useful items (And has &quot;Quick Cash&quot; as its colloquially called is otherwise worthless, I personally encourage you to either do that, or learn some Artificing yourself to make what you will.)<br /> <br /> In short, contrary to popular belief; you can indeed 'take it with you', you just won't be able to buy anything with it.<br /> <br /> The Arena Mortis<br /> <br /> When one is already dead, one has few qualms about taking a few hits here and there from a weapon. Wraiths don't bleed, and having a sword through your corpus is significantly less traumatic then a sword through one of the Quick. The Arena, simply put, is the place where Wraiths come to beat the everloving snot out of other Wraiths and dead things. The annals of Stygia claim that the Smiling Lord founded the Arena shortly after Stygia's founding, the records from the era indicate that he wanted a place where his Legion's members could find solace and come to terms with their death...By brutally cleaving their murderer's wraiths to bits.<br /> <br /> While this still happens, its not as frequent has it used to be. With the Tempest the way it is, shipping a single soul out to Stygia for the express purpose of beating it into a Harrowing and hoping it's too weak to resist its Shadow is VERY expensive. Of course, every so often the Smiling Lord decide's a Wraith's cause is sufficiently just to foot the bill himself, though some more cynical Wraiths believe he's more concerned with the idea of watching a particularly gory show then he is any consideration of justice. I shall defer to the reader on that subject, and encourage you to make your own judgement on the nature of the Arena Mortis.<br /> <br /> Of course, its not all revenge fantasies at the Arena. Just about any form of sporting combat can be found here as well, duelists who died while wielding flintlock pistols in the 1800s sometimes gather in the Arena to test their reflexes...Though given the short supply of relic bullets, this tends to be an uncommon occurrence, wealthier Wraiths sometimes manage to gather a few dozen for such an event. More common are the naumachiae, or &quot;Water Battles&quot;. Essentially its what happens when the entire Arena is flooded with Plasm from the Sea of Shadows, and ships are brought in for marine warfare. Due to the closeness of the Arena to the Docks, large cranes on the edge of the Arena are capable of bringing ships in and out of the Arena, and sophisticated pump networks ensure that the water level remains consistent. There is also Venatio, Arena Battles where powerful plasmics are turned loose against Wraithly warriors. A few Stygian Nobles have attempted to get captured Spectres brought in, but this has been met with a resounding statement of &quot;Are you really that bloody stupid?&quot; by Hierarchy officials.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The Zoo<br /> <br /> Founded by the current Laughing Lady, the Stygian Zoo is THE research center and exhibition of Plasmic 'life' as it where. All manner of exotic entities from the Tempest, the Labyrinth, and further shores exist within the Zoo, for the viewing pleasure of the citizenry of Stygia. In addition, moliated 'animals' exist in carefully constructed habitats, conditioned to mimic their existance in the skinlands, though this practice has more or less been abolished when the current Zookeeper took over in 2007.<br /> <br /> The Zoo, in addition to being a delightful place to spend ones evening watching the antics of Plasmics, Whistimmu, and other entities of the Shadowlands, is also the center of research on these creatures. The Zookeeper delights in finding out new things about them, and is much beloved by many of the more peaceful specimens, and even the more violent ones have a certain respect for him.<br /> <br /> Mr. Irwin takes good care of his wards, and has worked for the Zoo with the permission of the Emerald Lord since shortly after his death. In exchange for his work researching and handling the animals, the Emerald Lord made a public statement declaring that Steve's family was under his personal protection, any Wraith found accosting the Irwin Family will be, and I quote, &quot;Hammered on the forges and rendered into shapes so disgusting that to merely gaze upon them as one of the Quick is to hasten ones journey to the ranks of the Pentients&quot;.<br /> <br /> The Emerald Lord is generally considered to of been a fan of the Crocodile Hunter, as a result.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The Playwrights Haven<br /> <br /> A squat building sticks out of the end of one of the Entertainment Districts streets. The building is entirely walled with carefully folded pieces of paper, linked with moliate and artifice to ensure they stay strong and stable. The papers? All pages torn from the plays of Shakespeare, his work has had quite the impact on the Quick, and as a result, his plays tend to make it over here with regularity and remain intact for long periods of time<br /> <br /> The halls and rooms of the Tower are filled with Playwrights, those who wish to devote their unlives to the understanding of theater, drama, musicals, and all manner of stage-craft. A great theater in the round dominates the central courtyard of the tower, modeled after the Globe Theater's original rotunda, though far larger in size and scope. Here, actors rehearse their roles, and practice plays never finished, or that only existed in the Skinlands briefly within the minds of their authors, never put to paper and script. In the undercrofts, stagehands work with Artifice and Moliate to forge sets and scenes, and you can hear the cries of Chanteurs unleashing their Keening to thrill the heart and terrify the soul. Sandmen occasionally assist the stagehands, for a nominal fee, making illusions that bring out the magic in any play.<br /> <br /> Tickets to performances are generally somewhat expensive, but on occasion the prices are lowered significantly for exhibitions whenever the Stagemaster decides its proper to do so. A wealthy, and somewhat mysterious Wraith, the Stagemaster is known only by that title, having allegedly lost his original identity behind thousands upon thousands of masks, each a different role he found more pleasurable then his true identity. Whether or not he has truly lost his identity behind these roles, his is an actor without equal, and those few souls he takes under his personal wing may eventually gain the pleasure of hearing the Stagemaster refer to them as peers! An honor beyond measure for any of the actors of the Playwrights Haven.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The Pink Treasures<br /> <br /> I will try to be as...dignified as I can when talking of this...bawdy house. Founded almost immediately after Stygia, the Treasure, as its commonly called, is the city's oldest Brothel. You might be shocked to learn, dear reader, that the dead can indeed have sex. It just never results in procreation, and deviant acts that would either result in someones death or a lengthy jail sentence tend to become quite possible and relatively simple in the Shadowlands. I've never been, but I hear tell that they have upwards of a thousand working individuals within the halls of the Treasure at any given time, and all prostitutes in the city pay a stipend of their earnings to the Madame.<br /> <br /> The whores of the Treasure claim to of been founded by Cleopatra's wraith, but the accuracy of this is debatable. Frankly I'd imagine dead Egyptian Monarchs would have better things to do then whore around in the Underworld. Of course, if you're in Stygia for any length of time, you'll hear all sorts of stories about what any given famous persons Wraith did. &quot;Cleopatra runs the whore house&quot;, &quot;Gilbert-And-Or-Sullivan is the Stagemaster&quot;, &quot;I own an ashtray made of a werewolf who drank blood and cast magic spells&quot;<br /> <br /> ...At any rate, I digress. The Treasure is where you can find just about any sort of carnal pleasure, I've never been, but I hear tale of Moliate being used in disgustingly kinky fashions, phantasms of impossible creatures even by Shadowlands standards being called up for ones viewing pleasure. And while I've never been, I hear that some of the male prostitutes have even found a way to extend their...natural gifts significantly beyond what any in the Skinlands would be carrying around. There are rumors that wealthy wraiths can buy literally ANYTHING in the walls of the Treasure, though every so often Hierarchy officials conduct investigation and raids to cut out the more...unpleasant behaviors, ever since an orgy in 1965 ended with half a dozen Spectres running loose through the city. The Madame at that time was condemned to the forges, and since then, the Treasure has been considerably more &quot;Politically Correct&quot;.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Of course, this is just a small sampling of what the Entertainment District has to offer (I didn't even touch the Circus Moribund, or the Sportsman's Haven Gymnasium). I could devote an entire series itself to the myriad creative outlets in the district, but hopefully this small sampling will get you far enough along that you can whet your beak in the more pleasant side of being dead. For death may be the end of life, and it may not be the end of ambition...But nor is it the end of creativity, after all, if you play your cards right, you might well have eternity to refine your perfect novel, sing the perfect song, or dance the perfect dance. The next chapter details the Agora District, or the Market, which shares a border with the Docks and the Entertainment district. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Authors Note: I deliberately skimmed over some of the more unpleasant aspects of the Pink Treasure (Though I will say the in character author of this book has NEVER been to the Treasure and CERTAINLY isn't speaking firsthand about ANYTHING! Honest! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />). And as for the bit about Steve Irwin...well, I heard about his death on the radio on the way home from my grandparents when I was 16, and when I first learned Wraith was a thing, imagined him wrestling some sort of ghostly crocodile. And at that moment, I knew if I ever did anything creative with Wraith, I simply had to include the Crocodile Hunter. Godspeed Mr. Irwin, wherever you are, I hope you're happy and know your family is doing well!<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1645,"u